      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of an ellipse, we need to determine the lengths of the semi-major axis ($a$) and the semi-minor axis ($b$). We are given the coordinates of point $(1,1)$ as the midpoint of $\\overline{AB}$ and the lengths of $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{AC}$.\n\nWith this information, we can calculate the semi-major axis by finding the distance between points $(2,1)$ and $(2,-1)$. The semi-major axis ($a$) is the length of half of this distance:\n\\[a = \\frac{2}{2}\\sqrt{(2-2)^2 + (1 + 1)^2} = \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{4} = \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot 2 = 2\\]\n\nSimilarly, the semi-minor axis ($b$) is the length of half of the distance between points $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$. Using the distance formula:\n\\[b = \\frac{2}{2}\\sqrt{(1-1)^2 + (-1 + 1)^2} = \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{0} = 0\\]\n\nNow that we have the semi-major axis and semi-minor axis, we can find the foci of the ellipse. The distance from a point on the ellipse to each focus ($c$, the focal distance) is given by:\n\\[c = \\sqrt{a^2^ - b^2^}\\]\n\nIn this case, there is only one focus, and the distance from any point on the ellipse to this focus is $c$. This distance is given by:\n\\[c = \\sqrt{(2)^2^ - 0^2^} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is $2c = 2 \\cdot 2 = \\boxed{4}$.",
