      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to first identify the lengths of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\). The relationship between the semi-major axis \\(a\\), the semi-minor axis \\(b\\), and the distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus \\(c\\) is given by the equation \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\).\n\nIn the given ellipse, the center is at \\((h, k) = (1, 1)\\). The lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 6\\), respectively. Using these values, we can calculate \\(c\\):\n\n\\[c^2 = a^2 - b^2 \\Rightarrow c^2 = 2^2 - 6^2 \\Rightarrow c^2 = 4 - 36 \\Rightarrow c^2 = -32\\]\n\nIt appears there was a misunderstanding in the interpretation of the lengths of the axes. Let's correct it:\n\nThe major axis is the longest straight-line distance between any two points on the ellipse (along the major axis), and the minor axis is the shortest. In the given ellipse:\n\n- The distance between the points \\((1, 1)\\), \\((1, 7)\\) is the major axis, so the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is \\(\\frac{7-1}{2} = 3\\).\n- The distance between the points \\((1, 1)\\), \\((-1, 1)\\) is the minor axis, so the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is \\(\\frac{-1-1}{2} = 1\\).\n\nNow, we can find \\(c\\) using the relationship \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\):\n\n\\[c^2 = 3^2 - 1^2 = 9 - 1 = 8\\]\n\nSo, \\(c = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}\\), and the physical distance from the center to each focus is now \\(c = 2\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci is \\(2c\\):\n\n\\[2c = 2 \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} = 4\\sqrt{2}\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{4\\sqrt{2}}\\]",
